## 1. Orbital Astrodynamics (NASA JPL Standards)

### A. Vis-Viva Orbital Energy Equation
Determines the exact instantaneous velocity $v$ of a spacecraft at radial distance $r$ along an elliptical transfer orbit with semi-major axis $a$:

$$v^2 = \mu \left(\frac{2}{r} - \frac{1}{a}\right)$$

* $\mu = GM$: Standard gravitational parameter ($\mu_{\text{Sun}} = 1.3271244 \times 10^{11}\text{ km}^3/\text{s}^2$)
* $r$: Heliocentric distance of the spacecraft ($\text{km}$)
* $a = \frac{r_1 + r_2}{2}$: Semi-major axis of the Hohmann transfer ellipse ($\text{km}$)

### B. Hohmann Interplanetary Transfer $\Delta v$ Budget
Calculates the minimum-energy two-impulse maneuver between two circular coplanar orbits of radii $r_1$ (Earth = $1.000\text{ AU}$) and $r_2$ (Mars = $1.524\text{ AU}$):

$$\Delta v_1 = \sqrt{\frac{\mu}{r_1}} \left(\sqrt{\frac{2r_2}{r_1 + r_2}} - 1\right)$$

$$\Delta v_2 = \sqrt{\frac{\mu}{r_2}} \left(1 - \sqrt{\frac{2r_1}{r_1 + r_2}}\right)$$

$$\Delta v_{\text{total}} = \Delta v_1 + \Delta v_2$$

